<p class="kicker">Llama UI · package</p>

# Introduction

<p class="lead"><strong>@ggml/llama-ui</strong> is Llama UI as a package: the tokens, fonts, surfaces and components of Llama Chat, for llama.app first and the Llama apps after.</p>

## Where it comes from

The package lives in llama.cpp, in `ui/lib/ui`, and Llama Chat, in `ui/apps/chat`, is built on it: its tokens, fonts and base components come from here. Llama Chat's Storybook is still the full one, with the **Philosophy**, the **Brand**, the **Communications** and every component of Llama Chat. This Storybook has the same sections, the same toolbar and the same stories, cut down to what the package holds.

The tokens, the fonts and the base components are changed here, and Llama Chat takes them as they are. What both still keep a copy of, the Model UI among them, flows one way for now, from `ui/apps/chat` to here.

## What's in it

- **Foundations:** the primitive, semantic and component tokens, the type, corner, icon and control-height roles, the matte surfaces and elevations, and Llama Sans and Llama Mono.
- **Components:** the 29 base components Llama Chat is built from, Alert to Tooltip. A few have stories here so far; all of them are drawn under **UI** in Llama Chat's Storybook.
- **Model UI:** how a model is shown in Llama Chat and on llama.app: its name and badges, capability icons, maker's avatar and quant chips, under **UI › Blocks › Models**. They take model data in and report what the user did out.
- **Scrapbook:** proposals and variations, rendered before anything is decided.

## Using it

Import the theme right after Tailwind, then the components:

```css
@import 'tailwindcss';
@import 'tw-animate-css';
@import '@ggml/llama-ui/theme.css';
```

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import { Badge, Button } from '@ggml/llama-ui';
</script>

<Button variant="secondary">View on Hugging Face</Button>
<Badge variant="success">Downloaded</Badge>
```

Use the toolbar to switch between light and dark, and between the Paper and Matte light surfaces.
